\subsection{Motivation: Heterogeneity and Stragglers}
First, non-IID data distributions cause significant \textit{client drift}. When data is partitioned using a Dirichlet distribution with low concentration $\alpha$ (e.g., 0.5), local gradients diverge from the global gradient direction. Second, network instability leads to \textit{stragglers}. In environments described in \cite{kairouz2024advances}, waiting for all $K$ clients to complete computation introduces unacceptable latency. Ignoring slow clients (partial participation) can further skew the model if not handled correctly.

